---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const tiers = [
  {
    name: 'AGPL-3.0',
    price: 'Free',
    period: null,
    body: 'The complete SDK, for products that can meet the AGPL obligations. No feature is held back and nothing is time-limited.',
    detail:
      'You run your own infrastructure, you keep your own keys, and your source obligations are the licence text rather than anything we decide later.',
    cta: { href: '/licensing', label: 'Understand AGPL use', secondary: true },
  },
  {
    name: 'Startup',
    price: '$5,000',
    period: 'per year',
    body: 'Commercial production rights for one named product owned by one legal entity, purchasable without talking to anyone.',
    detail:
      'Licensed component: @open-e2ee/signal-protocol-sdk. Includes SDK updates during the term. Self-service annual checkout.',
    cta: {
      href: 'https://console.open-e2ee.dev/licenses/new?plan=startup',
      label: 'License for closed source',
      secondary: false,
    },
  },
  {
    name: 'Growth',
    price: '$20,000',
    period: 'per year',
    body: 'Commercial rights with a configurable product scope, plus implementation support while you integrate.',
    detail:
      'Contract-defined component scope, configurable product count, integration support. Quote and signed order form.',
    cta: {
      href: 'https://console.open-e2ee.dev/contact?plan=growth',
      label: 'Schedule a meeting',
      secondary: true,
    },
  },
  {
    name: 'Enterprise / OEM',
    price: '$50,000+',
    period: 'per year',
    body: 'Negotiated portfolio or redistribution rights, security review, and service levels.',
    detail:
      'Contract-defined component scope, portfolio or OEM rights, security review and SLA. MSA or signed order form.',
    cta: {
      href: 'https://console.open-e2ee.dev/contact?plan=enterprise',
      label: 'Plan a security review',
      secondary: true,
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pricing"
  description="The complete SDK is free under AGPL-3.0. Commercial production rights for proprietary products start at $5,000 per year."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container measure">
      <p class="label">Pricing</p>
      <h1>Free under AGPL. Priced when you need it closed.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        There is no trial, no feature gate, and no usage metering. The complete SDK is available
        under AGPL-3.0 today. You buy a commercial licence only when your product cannot meet the
        AGPL obligations.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="cta-primary" href="https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/quickstart">
          <span class="button">Build your first encrypted message</span>
          <span class="cta-sublabel">two clients · no account · no server</span>
        </a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="/licensing">Understand AGPL use</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <ul class="tiers">
        {tiers.map((tier) => (
          <li class="tier">
            <div class="tier-name">
              <h2>{tier.name}</h2>
              <p class="tier-price">
                {tier.price}
                {tier.period && <span>{tier.period}</span>}
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="tier-body">
              <p>{tier.body}</p>
              <p class="muted">{tier.detail}</p>
              <p class="text-links">
                <a href={tier.cta.href}>{tier.cta.label}</a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="muted measure">
        Taxes may apply. Prices are the annual list prices for the scope named above. The signed
        commercial agreement — not this page — controls the grant, covered products, support,
        renewal, and post-expiration rights.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container measure prose">
      <h2>What a breach looks like afterwards</h2>
      <p>
        The usual reason teams reach for protocol-level encryption is not a regulation. Almost no
        regulation requires end-to-end encryption; HIPAA, SOC 2, GDPR, and the rest are satisfied by
        transport security and encryption at rest. Anyone telling you otherwise is selling
        something.
      </p>
      <p>
        The real argument is what happens on the day your server is breached. If an attacker reaches
        your database and the message bodies there are ciphertext you cannot decrypt either, the
        conversation that follows is a different conversation. Several breach-notification regimes
        treat encrypted data differently from readable data — California&rsquo;s notification statute
        and Article 34(3)(a) of the GDPR both turn on whether the exposed data was rendered
        unintelligible.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are not your lawyers and this is not legal advice; whether any specific regime applies to
        you depends on your data, your jurisdiction, and your facts. But the engineering position is
        straightforward and worth stating plainly: you cannot disclose what you were never able to
        read.
      </p>
      <p class="honesty">
        This does not extend to metadata. Your relay still holds who talked to whom and when, and
        that is disclosable. <a href="/security">The full list is here.</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>How buying works</h2>
      </div>
      <dl class="definition-rows">
        <div>
          <dt>Startup</dt>
          <dd>
            Sign in, name the legal entity and the covered product, accept the commercial terms, and
            complete annual checkout. No call required.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Growth and Enterprise</dt>
          <dd>
            Portfolio rights, redistribution, integration support, security review, and service
            levels need scoping first. A short meeting, then a signed order form.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Evaluating</dt>
          <dd>
            Build the integration under AGPL first. Nothing about the code changes when you buy a
            licence — only the rights do.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href="mailto:licensing@open-e2ee.dev?subject=Commercial%20licensing%20question">
          Ask a licensing question
        </a>
        <a href="/security">Read the security model</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
